body:
There seems to be other files which are not being processed, for example

node_modules/@angular/common/esm5/src/pipes/date_pipe.js

```ts
var DatePipe_1;
DatePipe = DatePipe_1 = tslib_1.__decorate([
	Pipe({ name: 'date', pure: true }),
	tslib_1.__param(0, Inject(LOCALE_ID)),
	tslib_1.__metadata("design:paramtypes", [String])
], DatePipe);
return DatePipe;
```

@alan-agius4 ah, pity. It is related to that though, as the source that fails to be recognized is as follows:

```javascript
var DatePipe_1;
    DatePipe = DatePipe_1 = tslib_1.__decorate([
```

The intermediate assignment apparently fails to be recognized in ngcc's relfection host for ESM5, causing the decorators not to be linked with the class declaration.

I believe the error is in this method:

```
  protected getHelperCall(statement: ts.Statement, helperName: string): ts.CallExpression|null {
    if (ts.isExpressionStatement(statement)) {
      const expression =
          isAssignmentStatement(statement) ? statement.expression.right : statement.expression;
      if (ts.isCallExpression(expression) && getCalleeName(expression) === helperName) {
        return expression;
      }
    }
    return null;
  }
```

Not that if the statement is an assignment then we take the right hand of the assignment. This works in the case of 

```
DatePipe = tslib_1.__decorate([...]);
```

giving us the call to `__decorate()` but it does not work when there is a chain of assignnments:

```
DatePipe = DatePipe_1 = tslib_1.__decorate([
```
